over the past few weeks in afghanistan  we've been to secret schools  where young girls risk arrest doing  clandestine art classes  we've heard from scores of fathers  who've solved their kidneys just to feed  their families and then push their wives  into doing the same  [Music]  we've reported on badly burned toddlers  in hospital in danger of dying because  their families can't afford the bandages  we've seen the taliban's new  intelligence unit in operation  controlling the streets  and prisons crammed full of men and  children who say they've been put in  jail without proof or trials or any  evidence  we've seen the women demanding their  rights disappear  and their videos shouting for help  dismissed as faith the ministry of  interior absolutely rejected it  and we've witnessed long long queues of  people urgently needing food  and begging for help as the country  hurtles towards a humanitarian  catastrophe  but in a country with so many suffering  so much it was our journey to the north  west province of bad geese which shocked  the most  the winter chill has brought fresh  levels of suffering to a people who've  endured decades of it  but in one of the poorest countries its  porous region may well be the birthplace  of the world's largest humanitarian  disaster  five years old  seven years  nine years  six years old these are girls already  sold into marriage many too young to  even know their own ages  eight years they're often prompted by  their guardians  six years the husbands they'll go to are  a decade or more older the last to talk  is five years old  the youngest in her father's arms is a  seven-month-old baby given in lieu of  medical bills  the girls have no say  this in one of afghanistan's poorest  villages  and the deprivation here means girls are  being sold into marriage earlier and  more often  there's not enough room in the tent for  all the parents who want to show us  their young daughters they've sold  we don't want to sell them she says but  we're poor and we have to eat  hajira had fetched an above average  price more than six thousand dollars  then she got injured and the deal was  called off  i'd already sold her to a family her  father tells us but they brought her  back when she got injured and said they  couldn't take care of her so i never got  any of the money  now he's on the hunt for another buyer  once final payments made for his eldest  daughter she'll be sent to her new  family she wants to study and is too  young to question what's happening  my father sold me because we're poor she  tells us  that's what we're told time and again  and in this remote village it was hard  to find anyone who hadn't sold their  little girls as payment in kind to  settle debts or just to buy food  some of the villagers are now living in  tents after an earthquake a few days  earlier which has reduced to rubble what  little they had  you can see  all the house has fallen down all the  exterior walls have fallen down and the  two children sleeping there died these  people were struggling before but nearly  six months after the taliban took  control the country's economy is  collapsing  and those like gulsha who thought life  couldn't get any harder are telling us  it can and just has  she sold her two-year-old girl satera  who she was still breastfeeding her  family was so desperate  with no food no water and lots of others  to feed she says what mother wants to  sell their children but there's no other  solution  the baby was returned because she  wouldn't stop crying but the family who  bought her say they'll be back when  she's a bit older now gulsha is trying  to find buyers for her two other girls  and she's not alone the extreme poverty  across afghanistan has led to an upsurge  in early or forced marriages for money  they've all pretty much sold daughters  in this deprived region girls are  reduced to possessions to be bartered  over  the remote villages in this corner of  badgis are very conservative and very  underdeveloped  and they've long been strongholds of  taliban support  but the taliban commanders and elders  here told us  they want the international community  back  not the military but the foreigners help  so he's happy to see international  people back in afghanistan again  we want the international community to  come to afghanistan to help us the  commander tells us and since the taliban  came to power there's no security  problem for anyone and we assure all the  foreigners about this in the future  but right now the humanitarian crisis is  hitting the country hard and  afghanistan's daughters are amongst  those suffering most  so the women of badgis queue for hours  just for free paracetamol for the sick  in afghanistan's most deprived province  nothing comes easy and everything's  scarce  desperation is the only commodity not in  short supply here  they're having to cram babies onto beds  in the main provincial hospital  it's standing room only here  a measles outbreak is ripping through  them in the tiny with already poor  immunity measles can be a death sentence  the insecurities meant vaccines haven't  been given  and there are widespread claims it's the  taliban who prevented them  the number of measles here is very high  the doctor tells us we don't know how to  help them he says it's very hard for all  of us  the talib now in charge of health  services has no medical qualifications  and left school at 14. not so long ago  he was fighting foreign troops and  blames others for the current mess  everyone knows that previous president  ashraf ghani was the most corrupt person  in the most corrupt government in the  world he tells us and we have a lack of  everything  that means those who are least able are  losing this fight  erezzo breathed her last aching with  hunger and in a bitingly cold tent  her father traveled three hours to this  their nearest hospital  but no one can help his daughter now  the two-year-old's parents wrap up her  frozen body with the air of people with  no hope who never expected help and  didn't get him  we're living in tents her father says  some people don't even have tents where  we are there's no warm place and that's  why she died  there's no time for grief they've two  more children to keep alive  [Music]  there are too many la carreso who've  perished starving and cold  it never gets any easier  misery stalks every corridor and every  ward  she's already lost two other children  now rizzuma's youngest is only just  clinging to mine  cadia has a range of complications all  of them normally treated easily but  these are not normal times in a country  where little is functioning normally  right now  the baby has pneumonia measles a fever  and at the root of it all poverty and  little food  they bring in a nebulizer to help  [Music]  in a country where billions were spent  over two decades the young are suffering  terribly  later the same night after filming this  kadia also succumbed afghanistan's  babies are dying at a shockingly high  rate  in the maternity wards samaya and the  other teenage mothers edged towards  labour filled with fear  like asia she was sold into marriage at  14.  they're all living in a country with one  of the highest maternal mortality rates  every girl here knows surviving the  birthing experience will be one of their  toughest challenges  [Music]  finally trying to deliver her baby  there's no pain relief  there are few medicines at all here the  midwives must cut her if her baby's  going to be delivered alive  it's been a difficult and traumatic  delivery  and the baby is completely limp and  unresponsive  [Music]  i'm really scared the exhausted new  mother says  she's worried for herself and her  newborn son  she looks on as the midwives work fast  now  every second is going to count if this  baby is going to live  [Music]  he doesn't appear to be breathing  they frantically suck out the amniotic  fluid which is threatening to drown him  they have few medical tools to help them  save this baby  but my goodness they're using them as  best they can  they tie his umbilical cord  but he's not yet out of danger and still  not responding well he's taken to the  resuscitation room where there's a  rolling queue of babies needing help  they need to get more oxygen inside this  baby's lungs  but the hand comp is defective  most of these medics are working for  very little and have hardly been paid  since the taliban came to power and  foreign aid suddenly stopped  [Music]  but this little boy's battle to live is  their fight too both mother and baby  have somehow got through this but the  baby starts his life in a land where  most are hungry and the entire afghan  society is teetering on the edge of  collapse  alex crawford sky news

